# Task 22: line endings.
#
# Rule: text files are stored in the repository as LF and checked out with the
# platform's native ending - CRLF on Windows, LF on POSIX. Both a Windows clone
# and a POSIX clone therefore get sane line endings from the same bytes.
#
# Why this file exists: without it, `git add` printed
# "warning: in the working copy of 'X', LF will be replaced by CRLF" on every
# commit, because core.autocrlf was doing the conversion with no declared policy
# to check it against. The conversion also disagreed with what was already
# committed - the index held a mix - so the warning was telling the truth about
# a real inconsistency.
#
# Renormalization: one-time `git add --renormalize .` under these rules rewrote
# exactly one blob, src/main/java/algorithms/Array.java, which had been
# committed as CRLF. Every other tracked file already held LF and was left
# byte-identical. That commit exists on its own, with no content change bundled
# into it, because a line-ending diff applied alongside a real change would make
# the real change unreadable.

# Everything git can detect as text is normalized; binaries are left alone.
* text=auto

# The families the build and the docs are made of, stated explicitly so they
# normalize even if auto-detection is ever tightened or disabled.
*.java     text
*.md       text
pom.xml    text
.gitignore text
